WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.506
Monday, a water heater install. Tuesday, three drain calls. Wednesday, an emergency repipe.

00:00:06.636 --> 00:00:10.512
So here's the question. Which of those jobs actually made you money?

00:00:10.642 --> 00:00:15.170
Not the biggest invoice. The one that kept the most after labor and materials.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.962
Because one callback can erase the margin the original job was supposed to earn.

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:24.466
And without job-level numbers, you don't see it until the year is already over.

00:00:24.596 --> 00:00:27.890
If you run a plumbing business, the next few minutes are for you.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.101
Here's how the money side of plumbing usually works.

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:32.958
Jobs live in your dispatch app.

00:00:33.088 --> 00:00:34.964
Receipts for parts live in the truck.

00:00:35.094 --> 00:00:38.878
Service calls and installs get blended into one revenue number.

00:00:39.008 --> 00:00:43.641
So a drain clear and a repipe look the same, until you compare them on margin.

00:00:43.771 --> 00:00:48.023
Every tech feels busy. But whose hours produce the most profitable work?

00:00:48.153 --> 00:00:52.643
Memberships get sold once, then forgotten, until someone calls to cancel.

00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:58.536
A big install invoice sits unpaid past thirty days, while payroll and suppliers are still due.

00:00:58.666 --> 00:01:04.215
Then tax time arrives, and parts, trucks, tools and payroll costs are scattered everywhere.

00:01:04.345 --> 00:01:08.028
Your accountant asks for everything. And nothing's in one place.

00:01:08.158 --> 00:01:11.819
Busy all year, and still not sure what the business actually kept.

00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:15.795
That's not a work-ethic problem. It's a visibility problem.

00:01:15.925 --> 00:01:19.351
You already have the data: jobs, invoices, receipts.

00:01:19.481 --> 00:01:22.544
You just don't have one place that turns it into decisions.

00:01:23.094 --> 00:01:26.626
